      "id": "014",
      "headline": "Archaeologists Discover Ancient Delhi Traffic Jam Preserved In Amber, Dating To 1400 BCE",
      "summary": "The jam includes 47 bullock carts and one ox that appears to be attempting a U-turn.",
      "full_body": "A team from the Archaeological Survey of India announced the discovery of a perfectly preserved traffic jam encased in amber near Mehrauli, carbon-dated to approximately 1400 BCE. The specimen — described as 'extraordinary' in the ASI's press release and 'honestly not that surprising' by every resident of Delhi — contains 47 bullock carts, three palanquins, a panicked horse, and one ox in the middle of what analysts identified as 'a very ill-advised U-turn at a junction with no lanes.' A note scratched into the surface of one amber section, translated from an early form of Prakrit, reads: 'I should have taken the ring road.' Historians say this confirms Delhi's traffic culture is entirely consistent across 3,400 years.",
      "category": "world",
      "image_url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/factictionary/news-content/main/images/2026-06-06/014.jpg",
